A||N||7||rockcrawl||my trip to the salvage yard||||9||
Z||000000||rockcrawl||09-09-2000||09:55 PM||||I went to the local U-Pull-It yard last week and located a Muncie/Getrag in an '88 Beretta (for use in my 4.9 Ferrari conversion) for $70. I went back with a friend today, and guess what.... it was gone! I am very upset. Next time I won't wait so long. There was also an '88 coupe that I was going to snag the healight module and seats from, but it has been crushed. Isuzu 5 speeds were abundant, at least 3 or 4 in Fieros.  <P>The good news is that my buddy got some good deals on parts for his 2M4, including a polyurethane dogbone for $6, a K&N filter for $6. A/C hoses for $8, and some Boston Acoustics speakers for $3 each. I'll be going back for a perfect dash board for $10, some power window motors, and some nice quarter windows. I didn't have time to get them today, and I was really bummed about the trans.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||9
Z||000001||mrfiero||09-10-2000||12:08 AM||||My advice to you and anyone else who scours junkyards is to bring plenty of cash with you.  I always do when I hit the junkyards because I never know what I'll find.  Plus you run the risk of losing that part, whether by crushing or someone else beating you to it.  <P>It's too bad they crushed that '88 coupe.....I would have bought it!!  Oh well.<P>Good luck on your project.<BR> <BR>  <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000002||AL||09-10-2000||01:37 AM||||that's why i'm glad my junkyard takes atm,today i found a whole sub system 2 recall water strips and a couple switches for 20 bucks.<B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||09-10-2000||11:43 AM||||The nearest boneyard that ever has any interesting stuff is about an hour away <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.  They don't take plastic and I'm always a few dollars short to hook onto "the deal".  I'll have to make a trip out there in the next few weeks (not as much fun when it's 40*).||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||09-10-2000||12:04 PM||||Of of the four that I frequent (all within 20 minutes of my house) only one takes ATM.  For me it's not a problem of carrying enough cash, but having the right tools for the job.  I need to get a small toolbox together that has everything you need to disassemble a Fiero and just keep it in my trunk.<P>Monkey, I'm not sure what would be worse 40 degrees or 100 degrees.  I scored that Koni performance suspension (shocks, lowering springs and swaybars) when it was 101 outside.  After 1.5 hours in a mechanics jumpsuit, I was borderline dehydrated and close to heat exhaustion.||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||09-10-2000||12:27 PM||||100 degrees is definitely worse.  Actually, the worst thing is "100 degrees now, and it rained last night."  Trudge through mud, so you can get to the interior pieces on a car that's been sitting there closed up, acting like a solar collector all day!  <P>As far as the takealong toolkit goes, Sears makes pretty decent ones.  Mine (received as a gift -no idea what it costs) has sockets, screwdrivers, etc., plus a compartment in the lid for extra stuff (pliers, visegrips, ans so forth).  I take it with, for long trips - and for short trips to the local salvage yards.       ||||reg||9
Z||000006||HYPTNOTiSE||09-10-2000||12:59 PM||||Rock Crawl, You're from up near bethlahem?  I live in frederick, MD, and thats about a 3 our drive for me.  I'm looking for junkyards with fieros but can't seem to find any. Know of any south of you?  Thanks<P>Mike LeCompte||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||09-10-2000||08:12 PM||||Hypnotise,<BR> I live just south of Allentown/Bethlehem. I know a few yards in the Quakertown area, but thats not much further south. This weekend I was up in Hazleton at Harry's U-Pull-It <A HREF="http://www.wegotused.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.wegotused.com/</A>  . It's about an hour drive, but they claim to be the world's largest U-Pull-It yard  in the world . I'm not sure if I believe it, but it's the largest one I've been to. I won't go back without a dolly to wheel my tools around. They even have an on-site concession (sp) stand where u can buy hamburgers, etc.. Most of the cars are pretty well picked over very quickly, but they say their inventory is always changing.<BR> Most of the yards around here have at least one or two Fieros, Harry's had six or eight, maybe ten. There are a few more big places north of here that I'd like to check out, but I just don't have the time. <P>The reason I missed the trans was not because I didn't have the cash, It was because I stopped in on my way home one day and I couldn't pull it by myself. By the time I got somebody to go back with me, it was too late. The cool thing is that I drive a service van (company owned) that is basicly a rolling workshop. I carry all the tools I need and I travel all over eastern PA and western NJ. If I need a part I stop on the way home and get it, just don't tell my boss!<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||7
